# tools

Tool-side enforcement layer. Every model write passes through this
module; honesty guards and SpecDD Lever 2 Forbids enforcement live here.

## Must
- `write_file` and `edit_file` run honesty guards (role-path, placeholder, mass-deletion) before any I/O
- SpecDD Lever 2 `_check_spec_forbids` runs after honesty guards (cheaper guards first)
- `SddParseError` catch precedes `ValueError` catch in fs.py — `SddParseError` subclasses `ValueError`
- All tool errors return as `(str, str | None)` tuples; never raise to the caller
- `get_repo_root()` is the canonical accessor — never `from luxe.tools.fs import _REPO_ROOT`
- `cve_lookup` is gated to `task_type == "manage"` only (surface bloat regresses implement)
- New honesty-pattern guards land with a regression test in `tests/test_tools.py`

- The placeholder guard's prose exemption is CHAT-ONLY (2026-07-30):
  `make_prose_aware_write_fns()` skips `_check_placeholder_text` for prose
  extensions (`_PROSE_EXTENSIONS`: .txt/.md/.rst/.log/…) because in notes and
  session transcripts "# TODO: implement …" is user-dictated content, not a
  code stub. All other guards apply unchanged. Reachable ONLY via the chat
  front-end's write-mode extra-tool seam (same pattern as unrestricted bash);
  the module `TOOL_FNS`/`MUTATION_FNS` keep `prose_exempt=False`, so the
  benchmark/maintain path is byte-identical

- `bash` defaults to the hardened allowlist + chain/redirect rejection
  (`_validate_command`). The module `TOOL_FNS["bash"]` MUST stay restricted —
  every benchmark/maintain run uses it. The unrestricted variant
  (`make_bash_fn(unrestricted=True)` + `unrestricted_bash_def()`) is reachable
  ONLY via `luxe chat` dev mode (opt-in `/bash`, write-mode only), passed through
  `run_single`'s extra-tool seam; it never becomes the default fn

- `make_bash_fn` carries two further CHAT-ONLY seams (2026-07-31):
  `cancel` (duck-typed `.requested` — never an import from luxe.chat; tools
  must not depend on the chat layer) switches `_bash` to `_run_cancellable`,
  a Popen runner that polls the token (~0.2s) and SIGKILLs the process GROUP
  (start_new_session) so esc doesn't block on a hung command until the
  60/600s timeout; `on_start` fires with the command string BEFORE execution
  (dispatch-time visibility) and is exception-guarded — a broken UI callback
  never fails the tool. `cancel=None` (the bench default, and the only value
  `TOOL_FNS["bash"]` can produce) keeps the original `subprocess.run` path
  byte-identically; the timeout is enforced on both paths

- `_CHAT_EXTRA_ALLOW` (2026-08-05, currently `{gh}`) widens the allowlist for
  CHAT bash only: applied inside `make_bash_fn` (both restricted and
  unrestricted modes route through it; a no-op in the latter) and advertised
  by `restricted_bash_def()`. The module defaults — `TOOL_FNS["bash"]`,
  `tool_defs()`, and the rejection message with `extra_allow` empty — stay
  byte-identical: a benchmark that can reach GitHub's API is not
  reproducible, and the default description is golden-pinned. Remote
  mutations via gh ride GIT_WORKFLOW_HINT's explicit-ask rule

## Must not
- Bypass `_safe()` path scoping in any new tool
- Hardcode tool-allowlist subsets in `single.py` (the role config is the single source of truth)
- Wire unrestricted bash into the benchmark/maintain path or the default
  `TOOL_FNS` — it is a chat-front-end carve-out only

## Owns
- src/luxe/tools/**
